<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lauterecken</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lauterecken is a small town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kusel">Kusel</a> district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland-palatinate">Rhineland-Palatinate</a>, where the Lauter flows into the Glan. It belonged to the counts of Veldenz and from 1543 was the residence of the counts palatine of Palatinate-Veldenz, a branch of the Wittelsbach family, until their line ended in 1694.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lauterecken.jpg" alt="Lauterecken" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Olipet91 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:628_458_Lauterecken.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lauterecken lies in the North Palatine Uplands, in a hollow at the mouth of the Lauter, where it flows into the Glan, and at the mouth of the Grumbach, which also empties into the Glan. The town stands at some 170 metres above sea level, with hills on either side of the dales reaching about 300 metres, and lies roughly 20 km north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kusel">Kusel</a> and 25 km north-west of Kaiserslautern. Its municipal area measures 893 hectares, of which 307 are wooded. The dense settlement of the inner town on its slope goes back to the medieval fortifications, and the old centre was crossed by the Obere Gasse with its marketplace, now part of Hauptstraße.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Settlement here goes back to about 5000 BCE, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">New Stone Age</a> tools, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> hoard from the Schäferberg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> finds. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> occupied the area for more than three centuries, and after they withdrew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> advanced into it. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis I</a> was baptised, Christianization spread from missionary centres such as Hornbach Monastery, Remigiusberg Monastery at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kusel">Kusel</a> and Disibodenberg. The Counts of Veldenz, whose lands came through the Frankish Bishopric of Veldenz, held the estate of St. Medard am Glan, with Lauterecken and Odenbach.</p><p>Count Palatine Ludwig II introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> into the Duchy of Palatinate Zweibrücken, and the townsfolk had to convert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a>. After his death in 1532 his brother Ruprecht became regent for the underage Wolfgang, who later gave him his own county palatine, made up of the Ämter of Veldenz and Lauterecken. Ruprecht raised Lauterecken to a residence town and commissioned a large palatial castle. He died in 1544, leaving a young son, Georg Johannes I of Veldenz-Lauterecken, who fell into financial trouble and became entangled in a lawsuit before the Reichskammergericht that outlasted him.</p><p>In 1566 he added the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-petite-pierre">Lützelstein</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a> and later moved his residence there. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>'s wars of conquest the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reunions">Chambers of Reunion</a> demanded the union of Lauterecken with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, since it was still a landhold of the Bishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a>. The last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/count-palatine">Count Palatine</a>, Leopold Ludwig, opposed the demand and had to leave until the French occupation ended.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Lauterecken is the seat of the Verbandsgemeinde Lauterecken-Wolfstein and a state-recognised tourism resort, nicknamed Veldenzstadt after the comital family. Its listed buildings include the Catholic parish church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-xavier">Saint Francis Xavier</a>, built from 1848 to 1853, the Protestant parish church of 1865 and 1866 in the Gothic Revival style, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gebhard-leberecht-von-blucher">Blücher</a> memorial of 1936. Its calendar includes a spring market in May, a Folk Festival in August, an autumn market in October and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> market in December.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lauterecken” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their lands lay scattered in the Hunsrück, along the Moselle and in the western Palatinate around Meisenheim and <b>Lauterecken</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-veldenz">County of Veldenz</a></li><li><blockquote>By the Treaty of Marburg in 1543, Count Palatine Rupert, uncle and former guardian of Duke Wolfgang of Palatinate-Zweibrücken, received the old County of Veldenz with <b>Lauterecken</b> and lands in the Vosges, founding the line of Palatinate-Veldenz.</blockquote><a href="/state/palatinate-veldenz">Palatinate-Veldenz</a></li></ul></section>
